/**
 * @defgroup qt Qt integration
 * @brief Optional Qt 6 integration for libeasymcp2221++.
 *
 * The Qt integration is provided by the separate
 * `libeasymcp2221++-qt` library and namespace
 * `libeasymcp2221::qt`.
 *
 * It adds Qt-oriented convenience APIs without introducing a Qt
 * dependency into the core libeasymcp2221++ library.
 *
 * The module currently provides:
 *
 * - QByteArray convenience functions for I2C transfers.
 * - QByteArray convenience functions for SMBus block transfers.
 * - Qt meta-type declarations for selected core value types.
 * - ErrorInfo for transporting errors across Qt signal boundaries.
 * - GpioMonitor for event-loop-driven GPIO monitoring using signals.
 *
 * @par CMake
 *
 * Build libeasymcp2221++ with Qt support enabled:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake -S . -B build \
 *     -DLIBEASYMCP2221_CPP_BUILD_QT=ON
 * @endcode
 *
 * Consumers can request the Qt component using:
 *
 * @code{.cmake}
 * find_package(
 *     libeasymcp2221-cpp
 *     REQUIRED
 *     COMPONENTS Qt
 * )
 *
 * target_link_libraries(my_target
 *     PRIVATE
 *         libeasymcp2221::qt
 * )
 * @endcode
 *
 * @par Dependency
 *
 * The Qt integration requires Qt 6 Core. The core
 * `libeasymcp2221::cpp` target remains independent of Qt.
 *
 * @par Examples
 *
 * The repository contains three small Qt Widgets examples. They are intended
 * as programming examples rather than as a complete MCP2221 configuration
 * utility.
 *
 * @anchor qt_gpio_monitor_example
 * @par GPIO monitor
 *
 * See the @ref qt_gpio_monitor.cpp "complete GPIO monitor example".
 * It demonstrates the event-loop integration provided by
 * libeasymcp2221::qt::GpioMonitor. GPIO edge events are delivered
 * as Qt signals and used to update rising- and falling-edge counters without
 * an application-managed polling loop.
 *
 * GP0 through GP3 are configured as GPIO inputs at runtime. No configuration
 * is written to flash.
 *
 * @image html qt_gpio_monitor.png "Qt GPIO monitor"
 *
 * @anchor qt_pin_configurator_example
 * @par Pin configurator
 *
 * See the @ref qt_pin_configurator.cpp "complete pin configurator example".
 * It demonstrates using the strongly typed C++ pin-configuration API from a
 * small interactive Qt user interface. Each GP pin can be assigned one of
 * the functions supported by that pin, and GPIO
 * outputs can be initialized high or low.
 *
 * Applying a configuration changes runtime state immediately. The example
 * does not write configuration to flash and does not restore the previous
 * runtime configuration when it exits.
 *
 * @image html qt_pin_configurator.png "Qt pin configurator"
 *
 * @anchor qt_analog_lab_example
 * @par Analog lab
 *
 * See the @ref qt_analog_lab.cpp "complete analog lab example".
 * It demonstrates periodic ADC acquisition and interactive DAC control in
 * the Qt event loop. GP1 and GP2 are configured
 * as ADC inputs and GP3 is configured as a DAC output.
 *
 * ADC values are displayed as raw 10-bit readings and the DAC slider controls
 * the raw 5-bit output value, keeping the example independent of the actual
 * MCP2221 supply voltage.
 *
 * @image html qt_analog_lab.png "Qt analog lab"
 *
 * Together, the examples illustrate three common integration patterns:
 *
 * - libeasymcp2221::qt::GpioMonitor for asynchronous hardware events;
 * - synchronous libeasymcp2221++ operations driven by Qt user interaction;
 * - periodic hardware I/O driven by a Qt timer.
 *
 * The Qt integration library itself depends only on Qt 6 Core. Qt Widgets is
 * used by these examples only to provide small graphical front ends.
 */

